<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" dir="ltr" class="h-full bg-gray-100">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <%== assets(:css) %>
    <%== assets(:img) %>
    <%# Fathom rather than Google Analytics: no cookie, no personal data, no consent
        banner. Development and staging are kept out of the numbers by the allowed-domains
        firewall on the Fathom site rather than by a condition here, which is Fathom's own
        advice and is better than one -- it filters every hostname that is not the live
        one, including a local run that happens to boot with RACK_ENV=production.

        Deliberately not in views/oauth_layout.erb. That is the one page where a tap hands
        an API client the account, and the fewer origins in that moment the better. Since
        #142 that page's policy is default-src 'none' with no script-src at all, so this
        would be refused there outright; separate layouts mean leaving it out is the whole
        of it, and analytics_spec keeps it that way.

        It is the only third-party script the site loads, which is what this comment
        claimed while a tinyanalytics pixel sat twenty-two lines below it. Two vendors
        counting the same visits with different ideas of a session, different bot filtering
        and different sampling do not agree, so there was no answer to "how many people
        came this week" -- there were two, with no reason to prefer either. Fathom is the
        one #185 chose and said why: cookieless, no profile, no consent banner. The pixel
        was never part of that decision. analytics_spec now holds both halves, because the
        wrong half of this to get wrong is the comment: it is what somebody reads when
        deciding whether the CSP can be tightened. %>
    <script src="https://cdn.usefathom.com/script.js" data-site="VXQDPBHH" defer></script>

    <%# The title and the description, and what they are competing for. #359.

        They were `tectonic plates, a lifting app` and `Lift heavier with tectonic plates.
        Track your workouts and progress.` -- neither containing Claude, ChatGPT, MCP,
        connector or AI, while the visible headline has said "Ask Claude what to lift" since
        #269. #342 found that search returns no organic results for the literal string
        tectonicplates.app, and that "tectonic" belongs to a Minecraft mod. The metadata was
        pointing at the one term this domain cannot win.

        So it points at the terms nobody owns instead. It does not claim to be the first
        strength app an assistant can write to -- that line is taken, timestamped two months
        earlier, on a competitor that is free (#358), and claiming it invites the comparison on
        the one axis where the answer is "and theirs is free". What is defensible and unclaimed
        is the programme: weeks, days, progressions and warmup ramps, not a workout.

        **Both assistants, never one**, which is how this sits with #326. That issue decided no
        vendor is named anywhere a reader is being told what this app is, on the grounds that
        an app speaking an open protocol should not read as built for one client -- and it is
        right, which is why the headline still says "Ask your AI what to lift" and a spec holds
        it there. Naming Claude *and* ChatGPT is not the thing #326 refused: it is the opposite
        claim, that this works with whichever one you use. The two searchable terms #342 found
        unowned are "Claude workout connector" and "ChatGPT barbell program", and naming one of
        them without the other would be exactly the single-vendor reading #326 exists to
        prevent.

        The name being close to unsearchable is a larger problem than a meta tag and #342 flags
        a rename as a real option nobody has costed. This does not fix that.

        "Free while it is in beta" is #430, and it is here for a search reason rather than a
        marketing one: "free" is a word people put in the query box, and a description that
        omits it loses the comparison before anybody clicks. The clause is the same one the
        front page carries, because two surfaces making different promises about price is the
        one kind of drift this particular sentence must not have.

        A page may override both; every page that does not gets these. The default is a
        statement about the app rather than about whichever page is being rendered, which is
        what makes it the right fallback for forty pages behind a login that no crawler will
        ever see. %>
    <%- @page_title ||= 'tectonic plates — a barbell tracker Claude and ChatGPT can plan' %>
    <%- @page_description ||= 'A barbell strength tracker with an MCP connector. Ask Claude or ' \
                              'ChatGPT to write a whole training block - weeks, days, ' \
                              'progressions, warmup ramps, plate maths for your own rack - ' \
                              'then log every set against it. Free while it is in beta.' %>
    <!-- HTML Meta Tags -->
    <title><%= @page_title %></title>
    <meta name="description" content="<%= @page_description %>">
    <%# One domain. render.yaml serves this app at tectonicplates.app and still allows
        tectonic.onrender.com, so without this the same page is two pages. %>
    <link rel="canonical" href="<%= canonical_url %>">
    <%# The tab, the bookmark, and the iOS home screen -- which matters more here than on
        most sites, because a gym app that gets added to a home screen is a gym app that
        gets opened, and it was a blank document glyph until #254.

        Three files because browsers still disagree. icon.svg is the real one and scales to
        anything; favicon.ico carries 16, 32 and 48 for what cannot read it; and
        apple-touch-icon.png is opaque at 180x180 because iOS composites a transparent icon
        onto black. All three are the plates from _logo.erb with the wordmark dropped,
        since eleven characters cannot be read at sixteen pixels.

        theme-color paints the phone browser's own chrome, so the bar above the page is the
        app's blue instead of white. %>
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32">
    <link rel="icon" href="/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#075985">

    <%# Installable on a phone. #494.

        That issue asks for a rest timer in the Dynamic Island, which a web page cannot have:
        the Island is drawn by a Live Activity, and a Live Activity is ActivityKit plus a
        widget extension inside a compiled app bundle. There is no JavaScript binding for it
        and Safari vends no push-to-start token. The plan on the issue sets out the three
        routes and what each costs.

        This is the cheap half of the cheapest of them, and it is worth having on its own
        terms: an app on the home screen, opening without the browser's chrome, rather than a
        tab somebody has to find. It is also the prerequisite for iOS Web Push, which is the
        only way this app will ever interrupt a phone without a native wrapper -- though push
        needs a service worker and a subscription store, and neither is here.

        **iOS reads the apple- tags and not the manifest for this.** The manifest is what
        Chrome and Android install from, and modern Safari reads some of it, but standalone
        display and the name under the icon come from the two meta tags below. Both are
        present because the two platforms disagree about which to believe.

        `mobile-web-app-capable` alongside the apple one because the apple spelling is the
        deprecated form and the only one iOS honours -- dropping either loses a platform.

        status-bar-style `default` rather than black-translucent, deliberately: translucent
        puts the page under the clock, which wants a safe-area inset at the top that this
        layout does not have. Only the foot of the screen is padded for a notch today, in the
        RPE footer, and a heading sliding under the carrier name is a worse first impression
        than an ordinary status bar.

        The one real cost, worth naming: a standalone window has no Safari back button. Every
        screen here is reachable from the nav, so there is nowhere to strand somebody -- but
        that is a property to keep true rather than one to assume. %>
    <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.json">
    <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="tectonic">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="default">

    <%# Both cards fetch their image from this origin. They asked tectonic.onrender.com for
        it until #251, which is the sort of thing that keeps working until the day the old
        subdomain is retired and then breaks everywhere at once, silently, in somebody
        else's cache. The alt text is here because a card announced nothing at all to a
        screen reader, and og:site_name because it was attributed to nobody. %>
    <!-- Facebook Meta Tags -->
    <meta property="og:url" content="<%= canonical_url %>">
    <meta property="og:type" content="website">
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="tectonic plates">
    <meta property="og:title" content="<%= @page_title %>">
    <meta property="og:description" content="<%= @page_description %>">
    <meta property="og:image" content="<%= Tectonic::CANONICAL_ORIGIN %>/img/screenshot.jpeg">
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="A tectonic plates workout, with each set's weight and the plates that make it.">

    <!-- Twitter Meta Tags -->
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
    <meta property="twitter:domain" content="tectonicplates.app">
    <meta property="twitter:url" content="<%= canonical_url %>">
    <%# There is no separate product account, so the site and its author are one handle. %>
    <meta name="twitter:site" content="@stephanieblack">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="<%= @page_title %>">
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="<%= @page_description %>">
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="<%= Tectonic::CANONICAL_ORIGIN %>/img/screenshot.jpeg">
    <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="A tectonic plates workout, with each set's weight and the plates that make it.">

    <%# Charting costs a quarter of a megabyte, and on a page that draws no chart it is a
        quarter of a megabyte of nothing -- the login form, the sign-up form and every
        workout page carried it. A view that charts says so by setting @charts, which is
        set by the time this runs because Roda renders the template first and this layout
        around it. Chart.js is named before Chartkick, which adapts it. The vendored
        chartjs-adapter-date-fns bundle, 215KB of it, is not loaded for most of them: it
        teaches Chart.js to read dates for a time axis, and the volume charts plot their
        weeks as evenly spaced categories, so nothing there ever parses a date.

        **That day came with #434**, which is the chart the note above predicted: a lift's
        progress against a goal has a marker at a future date and a pace line running to it,
        and neither can sit on an axis made of the days somebody happened to train. A page
        that needs the calendar says so with @calendar, and only that page pays the 215KB --
        which is the whole of why the flag is separate rather than folded into @charts. It is
        also not the gym-floor screen: the session view draws no chart at all. %>
    <%- if @charts %>
      <script src="/js/chart.umd.js"></script>
      <%- if @calendar %>
        <script src="/js/chartjs-adapter-date-fns.bundle.js"></script>
      <%- end %>
      <script src="/js/chartkick.js"></script>
    <%- end %>
    <%# And the same for htmx, which is 50KB and was loaded on every page in the app while
        being used on three. /exercises, /volume, /programs and /about each parsed and ran
        it to do nothing at all -- and the front page and /about are the two most likely
        first visits, neither of which uses it.

        A partial that carries hx- attributes sets @htmx, so the answer is written where
        the attributes are rather than in a route that has to remember. The risk of getting
        this wrong is quiet -- buttons that stop swapping and fall back to full page posts,
        which still work -- so spec/htmx_loading_spec.rb walks the app and holds the two
        together instead of leaving it to be noticed. %>
    <%- if @htmx %>
      <script src="/js/htmx.min.js"></script>
    <%- end %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <%# First in the body on purpose: the first Tab press must land here, before the
        eight nav links a keyboard or screen-reader user otherwise steps through on
        every page. sr-only until it holds focus, so nobody else ever sees it. %>
    <a href="#main"
       class="<%= button_style %> sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:left-4 focus:top-4
              focus:z-50 bg-sky-800 px-3.5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white">Skip to content</a>
  <%== render('nav') %>
    <main id="main">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4">
        <%# Above the page rather than inside it, because a notice is about the request that
            just finished and not about anything the page is showing -- and because the page
            it lands on cannot know it is about to be handed one. The partial's own note has
            the argument, including why this app rendered no notice at all until #575. %>
        <%== render('_flash_notice') %>
        <%== yield %>
      </div>
    </main>
    <%# Asked once, after a sign-in by an account that has never said where it is (#349). The
        flag is set in after_login and cleared by the endpoint, so this is absent from every
        page of every session that has already answered -- which is all of them after the
        first. Last in the body because it changes nothing on the page and should not be in
        the way of anything that does. %>
    <%- if session['zone.detect'] %>
      <%== render('_detect_zone') %>
    <%- end %>
  </body>
</html>
